import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "Postgres",
	description: "Postgres on the rocx server platform: queries whose rows decode into the record you annotate, transactions that take jobs with them, migrations built into the app, and roc-pg, a client in Roc whose queries are checked when the app is built.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "query", label: "A query" },
		{ id: "rows", label: "Rows, as records" },
		{ id: "write", label: "Writing" },
		{ id: "transactions", label: "Transactions" },
		{ id: "migrations", label: "Migrations" },
		{ id: "where", label: "Where it can be used" },
		{ id: "roc-pg", label: "roc-pg: queries checked at build" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · Postgres"
				title="One database, and the types you say"
			>
				The server platform is opinionated: an app's data is in Postgres. <code>Db</code> runs a statement
				with its parameters and gives back the rows, decoded into the record the caller annotated; a
				transaction keeps one connection for a call; migrations run once each, when the server starts.
				The jobs queue and the actors' state live there too.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="query">A query</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.all!</code> runs a statement with its parameters and answers its rows. The type says what a
				row is; the platform's tests' app:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_notes.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_notes.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A parameter is <code>Null</code>, <code>Text</code>, <code>Int</code> or <code>Bool</code>, sent as
				text: Postgres reads it as the type its place wants (<code>$1</code> in <code>where id = $1</code> is
				a <code>bigint</code>). A statement is never put together from strings: its values go as parameters.
			</p>

			<Heading id="rows">Rows, as records</Heading>
			<p>
				The host reads each row's columns, as Postgres typed them, and hands Roc the rows as JSON;
				<code>Json.parse</code> decodes each into the record the caller annotated, by column name. A row
				that doesn't fit is <code>BadRow</code>, saying why.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Nulls are typed.</strong> A column that may be null is read as <code>Db.Nullable(a)</code>:
					<code>Null</code> or <code>Some(a)</code>, matched like any tag union. The record says what may be
					null, not the column's name or the table; a null in a column the record reads as a plain value is
					<code>BadRow</code>, which names the column.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What is read:</strong> booleans, integers, floats, text, <code>json</code> and
					<code>jsonb</code>, <code>uuid</code>, timestamps and dates (as text). Anything else asks for a cast
					(<code>::text</code>).
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="write">Writing</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.run!</code> runs a statement and answers how many rows it changed; <code>Db.script!</code> runs
				statements with no parameters, one after another, in one transaction (or in the call's, if it has
				one open).
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_insert.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_insert.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="transactions">Transactions</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.transaction!</code> keeps one connection for the call: what its body writes, and the jobs it
				queues, stay only if it returns <code>Ok</code>. An <code>Err</code>, a failing statement or a crash
				rolls it all back, and a call that ends with a transaction still open has it rolled back.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_transaction.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_transaction.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A job queued in a transaction is a row in it, so it runs only if the transaction commits, and the
				workers are woken when it does (<a href={Paths.platform_jobs}>jobs</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="migrations">Migrations</Heading>
			<p>
				An app's migrations are in its executable: <code>program.migrations</code> lists them, each a name and
				its SQL, which Roc reads from a <code>.sql</code> file when the app is compiled. When the server
				starts, the platform's Roc applies them in the order listed, each once, each in a transaction,
				recorded by name in <code>rocx_migrations</code>; two servers starting at once take turns (an
				advisory lock). The platform's own tables (<code>rocx_jobs</code>, <code>rocx_actors</code>) come
				first. One that fails stops the server, saying why.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_migrations.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_migrations.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={Sources.platform_migration.file} source={Sources.platform_migration.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} plain={Bool.True} />

			<Heading id="where">Where it can be used</Heading>
			<p>
				Anywhere on the server: a page as it renders, an endpoint, a job, an actor, a stream. So a component
				reads its data from a database through a GET endpoint: the handler runs only on the server, so it
				may query, and the browser's copy has only the endpoint's method and URL
				(<a href={"${Paths.calls}#user"}>who is asking</a>). A
				statement takes a connection from a pool of 16, and gives it back; a transaction keeps one until it
				ends. Statements are prepared once per connection, and kept.
			</p>
			<p>
				<code>just db</code> runs a Postgres in Docker and prints its URL; the server takes it as
				<code>--database-url</code> or <code>DATABASE_URL</code>. With none, a query answers that there is no
				database, and the server's other parts work. Connections use TLS when the database offers it (or the
				URL's <code>sslmode</code> asks for it), checked against the web's certificate authorities.
			</p>

			<Heading id="roc-pg">roc-pg: queries checked at build</Heading>
			<p>
				<a href="https://github.com/niclas-ahden/roc-pg">roc-pg</a> is a Postgres client written in Roc. It
				speaks Postgres's protocol over a socket the platform gives it (<code>Tcp</code>), logs in with the
				platform's <code>Random</code>, and checks each query against a schema when the app is compiled: a
				column that isn't there, a parameter of the wrong type, a column that may be null read as one that
				can't, each fails the build. The platform's example (<code>server/examples/roc-pg</code>) uses the
				same <code>schema.sql</code> as its migration, so what roc-pg checks against is what the server
				applied.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_rocpg.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_rocpg.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Use either, or both. <code>Db</code> has a pool, transactions that take jobs with them, and rows
				decoded into the record you annotate; roc-pg has queries the compiler checks, and a connection per
				use (it has no pool). Checking the example takes under a second from cold, and an optimized build
				about six; the platform's tests build it and run it against Postgres.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Db</code>'s queries aren't checked at build:</strong> a row is decoded by the record
					each caller annotates, and a mistake shows when the query runs. roc-pg's are checked.
				</li>
				<li><strong>Parameters</strong> are four kinds; arrays and bytes aren't yet.</li>
				<li><strong>No cursors or streaming:</strong> a query's rows come back whole.</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
